      "published": [
        {
          "claim": "three_cuts_bottom_36",
          "text": "A household that retired just before the Dot-Com Bubble burst would have hit its lower guardrail 3 times, ultimately bottoming out at 36% below the planned spending level.",
          "source": "Kitces (Derek Tharp), \"Why Guyton-Klinger Guardrails Are Too Risky For Most Retirees (And How Risk-Based Guardrails Can Help)\", kitces.com, March 2024, https://www.kitces.com/blog/guyton-klinger-guardrails-retirement-income-rules-risk-based/ — $1M, $43,000/yr, 60/40, retirement in 1999; quote verified against the page text 2026-09-26",
          "asserted_as": "exactly three capital-preservation cuts; the first in 2003 (after the 2000–2002 bear market) and the last in 2009 (after 2008); the deepest real cut lies within 36% ± 5 points (reproduction: 39.2% at G-K's own 5.8% / 50% equity)"
        },
        {
          "claim": "cap_binds",
          "text": "unmodified guardrails produced cumulative spending cuts of ~28% in 2008, ~36% in the dot-com bust, and ~54% through 1965–1975 stagflation … cap cumulative real cuts at 20%",
          "source": "07 §5.6, after Kitces 2024",
          "asserted_as": "with the 20% cap the worst cut is exactly 0.20 and cap_bound is recorded; without it the cut exceeds 0.20"
        },
        {
          "claim": "jeske_over_50_not_reproduced",
          "text": "Jeske also shows that January 2000 retirees, whether they began with 4%, 5%, or 6% withdrawal rates, would all have taken over 50% cumulative pay cuts during retirement.",
          "source": "Kitces 2024, citing Karsten Jeske (Early Retirement Now); verified against the page text 2026-09-26",
          "asserted_as": "NOT asserted. The annual Damodaran/BLS reproduction gives 25.3% (4.3%, 60/40) to 39.2% (5.8%, 50/50) through 2025. Jeske's series is monthly with his own horizon and allocation; recorded here so the divergence is visible rather than dropped."
        }
      ]
